## Iterating Over Vec Elements

The `for` loop is the idiomatic way to iterate over Vec elements. It's clean, readable, and handles bounds internally.

### Basic For Loop

```xiom
let fruits = Vec[Str]();
fruits.push("apple");
fruits.push("banana");
fruits.push("cherry");

for fruit in fruits {
  io.println(fruit);
}
// Prints: apple, banana, cherry
```

### For with Index (While Loop)

Since `for` doesn't directly give an index, use a `while` loop when you need positions:

```xiom
for item in vec {
  // item is the element — no index here
}

// With index:
var i = 0;
while i < vec.len() {
  io.println(i + ": " + vec[i]);
  i = i + 1;
}
```

### Processing Elements

```xiom
let numbers = Vec[Int]();
numbers.push(1); numbers.push(2); numbers.push(3);

var sum = 0;
for n in numbers {
  sum = sum + n;
}
// sum is 6
```

### for with Conditionals

```xiom
fn count_positive(v: &Vec[Int]) -> Int {
  var count = 0;
  for val in v {
    if val > 0 {
      count = count + 1;
    }
  }
  return count;
}
```

### for with Mutable Accumulation

```xiom
fn join(v: &Vec[Str], separator: Str) -> Str {
  var result = "";
  var first = true;
  for item in v {
    if first {
      result = item;
      first = false;
    } else {
      result = result + separator + item;
    }
  }
  return result;
}
```

### Ownership Note

The `for` loop borrows elements — the original Vec remains valid after iteration:

```xiom
let v = Vec[Int]();
v.push(1);
for n in v { io.println(n); }
// v is still valid here
```

